<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Commodus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/161">161</a>–<a href="/years/192">192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Commodus was Roman emperor from 180 to 192, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, with whom he had ruled jointly since 177. On his father's death he made peace with the Marcomanni and other Danubian peoples, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a>. His erratic rule, his appearances as a gladiator and his conflicts with the Senate ended with his murder on 31 December 192.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/commodus.jpg" alt="Commodus" width="320" height="507" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Merulana · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Commodo_vestito_da_Ercole_ai_Musei_Capitolini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Commodus was born on 31 August 161 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanuvium">Lanuvium</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> and Faustina the Younger. His twin brother died in 165 and a younger brother in 169, leaving him his father's only surviving son. He was the first emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/born-in-the-purple">born in the purple</a>, during his father's reign. He was with the army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a> in 172 and received the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>, and in 175, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avidius-cassius">Avidius Cassius</a> declared himself emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, he assumed the toga virilis on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> front. On 27 November 176 his father gave him the title Imperator, and on 1 January 177 he became consul at fifteen, the youngest up to that time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> died on 17 March 180, leaving Commodus sole emperor at eighteen. He soon made peace with the Danubian tribes. He had little interest in administration and left it to favourites: the chamberlain Saoterus, the praetorian prefect Perennis and the freedman Cleander. He devalued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denarius">denarius</a> and paid for handouts and shows partly by taxing the senators. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, a contemporary, saw his accession as a descent from a kingdom of gold to one of iron and rust; the Historia Augusta is unreliable.</p><p>Conspiracies followed. In 182 his sister Lucilla plotted against him; her accomplices were executed and she was exiled to Capri and later killed. Cleander sold senate seats and commands, and in 190 appointed a record 25 suffect consuls; a food shortage and a riot brought his fall. Commodus presented himself as Hercules. After a fire damaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 191 he declared himself a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> in early 192, renamed the city Colonia Lucia Annia Commodiana and renamed the months after his twelve names. He also fought as a gladiator in the arena.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In late 192 Commodus announced that he would take office on 1 January as both consul and gladiator. His mistress Marcia found a list of people he meant to execute, with her own name, the prefect Laetus and the chamberlain Eclectus on it. On 31 December she poisoned his food; he vomited it, and the conspirators sent his wrestling partner Narcissus to strangle him in his bath. The Senate declared him a public enemy and demolished his statues. His death ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nerva-antonine-dynasty">Nerva–Antonine dynasty</a>, and Pertinax succeeded him. In 195 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> had the Senate deify him. His sole reign is commonly thought to mark the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-romana">Pax Romana</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Commodus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…route from Malaysia to central Vietnam. Óc Eo, its port and entrepôt, has yielded Roman, Persian, Indian and Greek artefacts: Louis Malleret found there in the 1940s gold medallions of Antoninus Pius and Marcus Aurelius and locally made gold discs imitating coins of Antoninus, <b>Commodus</b> and Septimius Severus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Conspirators including his mistress Marcia and the praetorian prefect had the wrestler Narcissus strangle <b>Commodus</b> in his bath.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-31">31 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Marcus Aurelius died on the Danubian front, leaving the 18-year-old <b>Commodus</b> as sole emperor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-17">17 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Commodus</b> made peace in 180, and the Marcomanni remained a frontier people for two more centuries, with a queen, Fritigil, converting to Christianity around 396.</blockquote><a href="/state/marcomanni">Marcomanni</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanuvium">Lanuvium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.68,12.7&amp;year=181" aria-label="Lanuvium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4859 679 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Commodus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5309 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5309 949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.68,12.7&amp;year=181">What was here in 181 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
